  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nA large sprawling Hudson Bay high will keep the weather mainly dry\nthrough the early part of next week with some minor exceptions. \nThe first exception will be tonight when a mid level shortwave \ntracks through the western Great Lakes region, which could bring \nthe chance (20-30%) for light rain across central Wisconsin. Any \nrain from this shortwave would be very light, at most a few \nhundredths of an inch. The next exception to this dry pattern is \nthis weekend as a strung out low approaches the high and \nsubsequently gets pushed to the north and south as it approaches \nthe western Great Lakes. Rain chances have fallen all the way to \n10-20% Saturday night into Sunday morning as this system \napproaches the area and as was mentioned before it mostly likely \nwill remain dry for much of the area. The best chance would be \nacross western portions of central and north- central Wisconsin.\n\nThe best chance for a widespread soaking rain still appears to be\nduring the middle part of next week when the high retreats,\nallowing a low pressure system from the southern Plains to lift\nnortheast towards the western Great Lakes region starting on\nTuesday and lasting through the middle of next week. With PWATs \nreaching 90th percentile relative to climo and more concrete \nforcing present, initial thoughts point toward this being a heavy \nstratiform rain setup. Will continue to monitor trends throughout \nthe week. Otherwise, temperatures will read mainly in the 60s \nduring the extended, occasionally warming into the low 70s.",
